{"role":"assistant","content":"Baseline is a clean git tree. The static instructions are ~2.5k chars (~600-900 tokens depending on tokenizer) and the athlete context is tiny — the usage log shows each fact adds only ~15 tokens, yet the whole ~1000-token prefix gets re-created today.\n\nLet me quickly confirm the Anthropic cache-breakpoint rules (min tokens per breakpoint, max breakpoints) so the fix is technically sound.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_YAdiaCDgFJgCe8uQ7J1ZGxyY","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"Anthropic prompt caching multiple cache breakpoints minimum 1024 tokens per segment system prompt blocks\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_YAdiaCDgFJgCe8uQ7J1ZGxyY","content":"Title: What is prompt caching? How it works on Claude, GPT and Gemini\nSite: requesty.ai\nDate: 2026-11-01\nURL: https://www.requesty.ai/blog/what-is-prompt-caching\nSnippet: How does Anthropic prompt caching work? Anthropic makes you ask for caching. You mark the end of a cacheable block with `cache_control`, and the API caches everything from the start of the request up to that point. Per Anthropic's prompt caching docs, a request can carry up to 4 breakpoints, and the cache prefix is built in a fixed order: `tools`, then `system`, then `messages`. That order is the cache key in practice. Change a tool definition and the system prompt and messages caches are invali\n\n---\n\nTitle: OpenAI Prompt Prefix Caching — AI Engineering Academy\nSite: CoddyKit\nDate: 2026-10-07\nURL: https://www.coddykit.com/courses/learn_ai_engineering/openai-prompt-prefix-caching-10619599\nSnippet: Anthropic Prompt Caching Anthropic offers a similar feature called prompt caching for Claude models, but requires explicit opt-in by marking cache breakpoints in the prompt with a `cache_control` field. Unlike OpenAI's automatic caching, you explicitly mark which portions of the prompt should be cached (up to 4 cache breakpoints per request). Cached tokens cost 10 percent of the standard input price and are stored for 5 minutes. ``` import anthropic client = anthropic.Anthropic() LONG_DOCUMENT =\n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt caching: how it works and how to cut LLM token costs\nSite: mastra.ai\nDate: 2026-08-17\nURL: https://mastra.ai/articles/prompt-caching\nSnippet: Implementing prompt caching across LLM providers Anthropic Claude cache control You control caching directly with Anthropic Claude. The API uses cache_control annotations, specifically `\"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`, placed on the message blocks you want stored. Without those markers, even a perfectly static system prompt is reprocessed every call because the client never asked for it to be cached. Here is a minimal example of how you annotate a message block for Anthropic Claude: ``` \n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt Caching: Decision Framework for LLM Cost, Latency, and Security (2026)\nSite: templates\nDate: 2026-10-08\nURL: https://explainx.ai/blog/prompt-caching-llm-cost-optimization-2026\nSnippet: Provider Mechanics: Anthropic vs OpenAI Both major providers implement prefix caching, but the control surface differs: table · 3 cols |Aspect|Anthropic (Claude)|OpenAI| |-|-|-| |Control|Explicit `cache_control` breakpoints on content blocks|Automatic on eligible requests| |Minimum size|Varies by model; typically 1,024+ tokens per cache block|Automatic caching above 1,024 tokens| |Pricing|Separate cache write vs cache read rates|Cached input at reduced rate on supported models| |TTL|Configurable\n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt Caching: How It Works, Provider Pricing ... - Morph\nSite: Morph AI\nDate: 2026-09-01\nURL: https://www.morphllm.com/prompt-caching\nSnippet: Anthropic and OpenAI will not cache prompts under 1,024 tokens (4,096 on Gemini 3.x and Claude Haiku 4.5). A 300-token prompt has nothing to ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt Caching\nSite: 百度百科\nDate: 2026-06-30\nURL: https://baike.baidu.com/en/item/Prompt%20Caching/2682745\nSnippet: 1 Chinese name 提示缓存 Definition Prompt Caching is an optimization feature introduced by Anthropic for its Claude model. This feature allows developers to reuse static content from prompts across multiple API calls, aiming to avoid reprocessing prompts that have already been processed, thereby reducing token consumption and improving response speed. 4 Core Technology At the engine layer, the core component is a QueryEngine singleton. It is responsible for concatenating context, managing prompt cac\n\n---\n\nTitle: Anthropic\nSite: kagent.dev\nDate: 2026-10-23\nURL: https://kagent.dev/docs/kagent/1.x/setup/model-providers/anthropic/\nSnippet: Prompt caching Permalink for this section An agent that calls a model many times for one task resends the same prefix every time, including the tool definitions, the system prompt, and the turns already taken. When `promptCaching` is `true`, kagent marks that prefix with `cache_control` breakpoints, and Anthropic bills a later request that reuses it at a fraction of the normal input price. Because the conversation breakpoint moves with every turn, each call in an agent loop reads the whole previ\n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt Caching Deep Dive: OpenAI, Anthropic, Google\nSite: Itsourcecode.com\nDate: 2026-10-15\nURL: https://itsourcecode.com/web-dev/prompt-caching-deep-dive/\nSnippet: Anthropic prompt caching Explicit. You mark cache breakpoints in your request with `cache_control` blocks. Everything before a breakpoint is cached as a block. ``` from anthropic import Anthropic client = Anthropic() response = client.messages.create( model=\"claude-sonnet-5-5\", max_tokens=1024, system=[ { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"You are a code reviewer. Here is our style guide: ...\", \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}, }, ], messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Review this diff:\\n\\n\" + di\n\n---\n\nTitle: Direct providers: controls, caching and accounting\nSite: Octipus\nDate: 2026-09-01\nURL: https://octipus.cc/docs/features/direct-providers/\nSnippet: Caching and native protocols Section titled “Caching and native protocols” Anthropic: native Messages is the default. Stable system-prompt prefixes use an explicit cache breakpoint, and a second breakpoint marks the settled conversation history — the last non-system turn before the newest one — so an agent loop’s accumulated tool results are re-read at cache rates instead of full price on iterations 2..N ( `markHistoryCacheBreakpoint` in `src/models/providers/custom/anthropic-compat-provider.ts`\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cache hit vs cache miss: latency benchmarks across providers · n4n AI\nSite: n4n AI\nDate: 2026-07-17\nURL: https://n4n.ai/blog/cache-hit-vs-cache-miss-latency-benchmarks-across-providers/\nSnippet: Ergonomics Anthropic’s explicit `cache_control` is the most predictable. You know exactly what is cached and can place multiple breakpoints (up to 4). The downside is you must structure requests as content blocks, not a flat string. Gemini’s out-of-band cache is clean for static corpora but adds a management step: create, poll, delete. If your context changes hourly, you own that lifecycle. OpenAI’s automatic caching is zero-code but opaque. You must ensure byte-exact prefix match including whit\n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt Caching for Real-World LLM Apps\nSite: essamamdani.com\nDate: 2026-08-11\nURL: https://essamamdani.com/blog/prompt-caching-real-world-llm-apps\nSnippet: OpenAI and Anthropic solve the same problem differently |Dimension|OpenAI|Anthropic| |-|-|-| |Main control surface|`prompt_cache_key` plus `prompt_cache_retention` on older families|`cache_control` on cached blocks plus explicit breakpoints| |What gets reused|Long prompt prefixes that match a stable prefix hash|Prompt blocks marked for caching, including system, tool, and message blocks| |Minimum useful size|Docs say caching is available for prompts containing 1024 tokens or more|Best for long, \n\n---\n\nTitle: Prompt Caching Across OpenAI, Claude, and Gemini - AveMujica API Blog\nSite: AveMujica API\nDate: 2026-07-15\nURL: https://api-cn.avemujica.moe/blog/prompt-caching-openai-claude-gemini\nSnippet: Anthropic: a `cache_control` marker, automatic or explicit Anthropic caches only what a `cache_control` marker covers, but you do not have to hand-place every breakpoint. There are two modes. Automatic caching adds a single `cache_control` field at the top level of the request; the system applies the breakpoint to the last cacheable block and moves it forward as the conversation grows, which is the low-effort choice for multi-turn chats. Explicit breakpoints put `cache_control` directly on indiv\n\n---\n\nTitle: Optimizing Claude Latencies using Prompt Caching at the Edge\nSite: NeelTech Portfolio & Engineering Insights\nDate: 2026-09-10\nURL: https://www.neeltech.me/blog/anthropic-prompt-cache-serverless-edge-nextjs\nSnippet: Understanding Cache Breakpoints # According to the Anthropic Prompt Caching documentation, you must define \"breakpoints\" within your message array. A breakpoint tells the API: \"Everything before this point is static and should be stored.\" The Anatomy of a Cached Request # When structuring your payload, you insert a `cache_control` object into the content block. |Component|Role| |-|-| |System Prompt|The primary candidate for caching (static instructions).| |RAG Context|Large knowledge bases that \n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
